\title{The algebraic branch: interpolation to rank sums}
\taxon{Overview}
\meta{dependencies}{None.}
\meta{provenance}{Expository organization and source review, 9 October 2026.}

\p{This argument concerns subspaces of a finite-dimensional real vector space and polynomials with vector coefficients. Choose distinct real numbers at which to impose conditions on polynomial values; these evaluation points are called [interpolation nodes](cn-0063). The route is evaluation dimensions, rank loss at those points, multiplicity of polynomial determinants, and a degree count. Graphs of linear maps then turn intersection dimensions into matrix ranks.}

